/* Ultra: a meteor shower crosses the track. Each meteor is a gradient streak with a

   bright leading point; the eleven of them run on negative delays, so the field is full

   from the first painted frame instead of filling in over two seconds. Track width is

   measured from the shell by the component, and this value is only the fallback for the

   frames before that measurement lands. It is a pixel distance on purpose: `translateX`

   resolves a percentage against the meteor's own width (14-42px), so `calc(100% + 36px)`

   here let every streak die less than a third of the way along the rail. An extra sweep

   runs along the track edge at maximum, and everything collapses to a static sheen under

   reduced motion. */

/* No `overflow: hidden` on the shell any more. It used to clip the meteors, but it also clipped the knob:

   while dragging, the knob grows to 1.08× and at either end of the travel it stuck out past the shell and

   was sliced — which is what the owner sees as "pulling it to the end does not line up". The clip now lives

   on the meteor layer alone (`.cg-model-meteors`), which is what the reference plugin does: its groove

   clips, its knob is a sibling and may overhang. */

.cg-model-slider-shell { --cg-meteor-travel: 420px; }

/* One extra px of room each side, not four: the halo below is painted inside this box, so whatever

   the box refuses to show is what gets clipped. At `inset: 4px` the visible height was 20px against

   streaks sitting at y=3…18, which left no streak enough clearance for a 7px halo — it was painted

   and then cut off. This buys 26px without letting the streaks touch the shell edge. */

.cg-model-meteors { position: absolute; inset: 1px 0; overflow: hidden; z-index: 1; border-radius: inherit; pointer-events: none; }

/* A screen blend used to sit on this container, and it is gone on purpose. It bought +20% to +35%

   relative luminance across the tail — genuinely the cheapest brightness per *painted* pixel — but it

   was not cheap per *frame*: a blended element cannot be composited on its own, it has to be recomposited

   against its backdrop every time either side changes, and this layer's own content changes on every

   frame because eleven streaks translate across it. The cost therefore scaled with frames, not with

   paint: the layer fell off the GPU fast path and every streak's transform dragged a full re-blend of

   the 26px band behind it. The brightness is bought back in the places that cost nothing per frame —

   the tail's own gradient alphas below and the halo's alpha in `.cg-model-meteor` — because a static

   gradient is rasterised once and then only translated. Dark theme lost the most, so the compensation

   lives on the dark branch and the light branch keeps its own saturated values. */

.cg-model-meteor {

  position: absolute; top: var(--cg-meteor-y); left: 0; display: block;

  width: var(--cg-meteor-len); height: var(--cg-meteor-thick);

  transform-origin: left center;

  /* This box paints nothing, and it carries no clip. The streak and the taper live one box down, on

     `::before`, for one measured reason: `clip-path` clips the element *and its pseudo-elements* and

     the filter result with them (Masking 1 §2 — filter, then clip, then mask, then opacity). With the

     clip on this box the drop-shadows were painted from the pill and then thrown away: sampled in

     Chrome, every halo pixel outside the box measured 0, and only 1.5% of the shadow pixels survived,

     all of them inside the box. That is the "怎么不发光了": the streak lost its light, not its motion.

     The tail keeps its own colour ramp — only the leading point was ever recoloured, because a

     purple head on a warm tail reads as a stray dot rather than the tip of the streak. The head

     below is a soft translucent white instead of an opaque one, so it sits inside the tail's

     brightest stop rather than stamping a hard dot onto it.

     White, and now actually white. The streak's `::before` paints two background layers — a white ramp

     over a tier-coloured one — and the colour layer used to sit at 34/62/74%, which read as a purple

     (Ultra) or gold (超快) bar with white laid on top, not as the white blocks the owner asked for. It

     sits at 10–18% now: a trace of air in the tail, not a tint. Nothing about the glow is lost, because

     the glow never came from that layer — it is the drop-shadow on `.cg-model-meteor` and the head below.

     The reference version the owner remembers is a field of small white light blocks crossing the rail —

     short, bright — and a long ramp of purple-to-gold reads as tape next to it. The mode colour lives in

     the head, which is where it belongs: the streak is light, the tip is the tier. */

  background: none;

  /* One halo, painted from `::before`'s *clipped* silhouette, so it follows the taper and fades with

     the tail instead of ringing a rectangle — the same shape the old bloom band had, which is why the

     band could go. It used to be two stacked drop-shadows (a 3px white one under a 5px mode-coloured

     one); that pair is the expensive half of this element, because the head below pulses inside this

     filter, so the filter re-runs whenever the head does, and eleven streaks then cost twenty-two blur

     rasterisations per frame. One pass at the intermediate radius costs eleven and reads the same at 3px

     thickness. The colour is the old pair collapsed into one: mostly white with the tier colour mixed

     in, at an alpha a little above the 0.685 the two translucent layers stacked to — that is the

     compensation for the screen blend this layer no longer gets, see the note above this block.

     Radius stays small on purpose: the layer is only 26px tall and its overflow clips whatever a bigger

     blur spreads past it, so a wide radius would be painted and then thrown away. */

  filter: drop-shadow(0 0 4px color-mix(in srgb, var(--cg-glow, #ffffff) 38%, #ffffffa6));

  opacity: 0; animation: cg-model-meteor var(--cg-meteor-dur) linear var(--cg-meteor-delay) infinite; animation-duration: calc(var(--cg-meteor-dur) * 1);

}

/* The leading point: brighter, round, and slightly ahead of the tail. Its full rule — geometry,

   colour and the four-shadow halo — is stated once below, after the streak it sits on. */



/* ── The streak, and the bloom it now carries inside itself ────────────────────────────────────

   The streak is a white bar: full thickness at the leading (right) end, about two thirds of it at

   the tail. Tapered, not a cylinder — a true point would read as a comet rather than the short bar

   of light this is meant to be, so the tail only thins; reading right-to-left, the streak narrows a

   little the further you get from the tip. Done with clip-path rather than a height change because

   the height comes from a data variable.

   The clip lives here and nowhere else. This box *is* the shape, so clipping it costs nothing else:

   the element above stays unclipped and can still paint the halo, which is the arrangement that was

   missing before. Everything this box draws is inside the trapezoid, so the bloom that used to be a

   band 2px/6px outside the box is now the second background layer, under the bar — it colours the

   thin tail instead of haloing it, and the halo itself is the element's drop-shadow. */

.cg-model-meteor::before {

  content: ''; position: absolute; inset: 0; border-radius: 999px;

  clip-path: polygon(0% 17%, 100% 0%, 100% 100%, 0% 83%);

  /* Two layers, and the second one is a trace rather than a tint. The owner's picture is a field of

     white light blocks, so the streak's body has to read as white; the tier colour belongs to the head

     below and to the drop-shadow on `.cg-model-meteor`, which is where the glow actually comes from.

     The colour layer stays at 10–18% for the faint air it gives the tail and nothing more. At the

     34/62/74% it used to carry, the streak read as a purple (Ultra) or gold (超快) bar with white laid

     over it — never the white the request asked for. Dark branch only: the light branch restates this

     whole list below with its own values. */

  background-image:

    linear-gradient(90deg, #ffffff00, #ffffff8f 20%, #fffffff0 70%, #ffffff),

    linear-gradient(90deg,

      #ffffff00 0%,

      color-mix(in srgb, var(--cg-glow, #c9b4f0) 10%, transparent) 38%,

      color-mix(in srgb, var(--cg-glow, #c9b4f0) 14%, transparent) 78%,

      color-mix(in srgb, var(--cg-glow, #c9b4f0) 18%, transparent) 100%);

}



/* Head: a flare plus a slow breath. The point stays 2px on purpose — at that size a 2px offset inside a

   3px blur overlaps into one soft cross, which is the look a star wants; a conic gradient would need a

   bigger box, and enlarging the box is what turns a tip into a shape.

   Four layers, not six. The two that went are the vertical arms at 38% — the weakest pair, and the shaft

   they would add is the direction the streak already travels in, so on a 2px dot they were doing the least

   work of anything here while still costing two more blurred shadows per head. The 58% ring came up to 62%

   to hold the head's overall light after the layer blend was removed. Everything rides --cg-glow, so 超快

   keeps its gold head and the Ultra tiers their purple with no second rule. */

/* The head, in full and in one place. It used to be split across two blocks — geometry and a

   single-shadow halo up in the streak section, then four shadows down here — and the second block's

   box-shadow silently replaced the first one's, leaving a dead declaration behind. Merged.

   The head wears the mode's own colour: --cg-glow is the tier glow this panel already breathes in,

   #f7c948 gold under superfast and #bb74ff purple at the top non-superfast stop. Mixed toward white so

   it stays a soft tip instead of a saturated dot; the plain declaration in front is the fallback for a

   browser without color-mix, matching how the popover halo degrades. Every var() carries #ffffffb3 as

   its fallback because this rule also runs where --cg-glow has no tier (off / none) and must stay white

   rather than losing the declaration. */

.cg-model-meteor::after {

  content: ''; position: absolute; right: -1px; top: 50%; width: 2px; height: 2px;

  margin-top: -1px; border-radius: 50%;

  background: var(--cg-glow, #ffffffb3);

  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--cg-glow, #ffffffb3) 45%, #ffffff);

  box-shadow:

    0 0 5px 0         color-mix(in srgb, var(--cg-glow, #ffffffb3) 62%, transparent),

    0 0 2px 0         color-mix(in srgb, var(--cg-glow, #ffffffb3) 46%, #ffffff),

    2px 0 2px -.5px   color-mix(in srgb, var(--cg-glow, #ffffffb3) 46%, transparent),

    -2px 0 2px -.5px  color-mix(in srgb, var(--cg-glow, #ffffffb3) 46%, transparent);

  animation: cg-model-meteor-head 1.15s ease-in-out infinite;

}

/* Opacity and transform only: both are composited, so the painted shadows are cached and the pulse

   costs no repaint. A keyframed box-shadow would repaint eleven heads on every frame. */

@keyframes cg-model-meteor-head {

  0%, 100% { opacity: .8;  transform: scale(1); }

  50%      { opacity: .94; transform: scale(1.12); }

}



/* Light theme: the rail is near-white (#e8e6e8), where added white is nothing, so the flare and the

   bloom lean on the mode colour instead. Both background layers are restated: a background-image

   override replaces the whole list, and dropping the first one would make the streak itself vanish in

   the light theme. The colour layer carries the same low alpha as the dark branch — the streak has to

   read as white in both themes, and on this rail that means the white ramp does the work. */

body:not([data-ds-dark-theme]) .cg-model-meteor::before {

  background-image:

    linear-gradient(90deg, #ffffff00, #ffffff80 20%, #ffffffe6 70%, #ffffff),

    linear-gradient(90deg,

      #ffffff00 0%,

      color-mix(in srgb, var(--cg-glow, #8f74d6) 8%, transparent) 38%,

      color-mix(in srgb, var(--cg-glow, #8f74d6) 12%, transparent) 78%,

      color-mix(in srgb, var(--cg-glow, #8f74d6) 16%, transparent) 100%);

}

/* The shared halo leans white so it survives on the dark rail; on the light rail a near-white blur is

   nothing, so light keeps the mode colour the way its flare does. Same single pass, same radius. */

body:not([data-ds-dark-theme]) .cg-model-meteor {

  filter: drop-shadow(0 0 4px color-mix(in srgb, var(--cg-glow, #8f74d6) 58%, transparent));

}

body:not([data-ds-dark-theme]) .cg-model-meteor::after {

  box-shadow:

    0 0 7px 1px       color-mix(in srgb, var(--cg-glow, #8f74d6) 74%, transparent),

    0 0 2px 0         var(--cg-glow, #8f74d6),

    2px 0 3px -.5px   color-mix(in srgb, var(--cg-glow, #8f74d6) 54%, transparent),

    -2px 0 3px -.5px  color-mix(in srgb, var(--cg-glow, #8f74d6) 54%, transparent);

}



/* Reduced motion: the block further up already stops every animation and parks the shower at .45,

   which leaves all eleven streaks stacked at the left edge. `::before` is the streak itself now, so

   there is nothing to hide there; the head keeps a single small halo. */

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {

  .cg-model-meteor::after {

    animation: none; transform: none;

    box-shadow: 0 0 5px 0 color-mix(in srgb, var(--cg-glow, #ffffffb3) 64%, transparent);

  }

}

/* Eleven streaks, every gap under ~0.4s and none of the periods a multiple of another, so

   the field never falls into a visible rhythm; the short ones read as fast dust and the long ones as

   the occasional real meteor. Lengths run 15/16/18/20/23/24/26/28/30/32/34px — a 2.27× spread end to end,

   scattered across the lanes rather than sorted, so adjacent streaks never pair up at the same size.

   What a streak is graded against is not L/T but the straight edge it keeps: `border-radius: 999px` eats

   T/2 at each end, so the flat run is L − T, and the ratio that decides "bar or dot" is (L − T) / T ≥ 4 —

   equivalently L ≥ 5T. L/T = 4 is not enough: 12/3 clears it and still leaves 9/3 = 3.00 of straight edge,

   which is the "豆" the owner reported. Measured at DPR 1.25 against a rasterised box, the straight edge

   covers 0.77–0.87 of the length at L/T = 4 but only 0.57–0.63 at 2, so the gap is real.

   All eleven now satisfy L ≥ 5T (15/3 leaves 4.00 straight, 34/4 leaves 7.50), and the short ones carry

   the thinner body so the field still reads as one system rather than eleven unrelated sizes.

   Thickness is integer only: measured, 3.5px rasterises as 3px plus one 38%-coverage edge row, while

   4px fills that row to 79% — a 2.5/3.5 split would be invisible. Two body sizes, length does the grading.

   34px is the ceiling: past that a streak stops reading as a short bar of light and turns back into the

   long coloured tape that was already rejected once.

   Duration, delay and lane are untouched — that rhythm was accepted. */

.cg-model-meteor:nth-child(1) { --cg-meteor-y: 3px; --cg-meteor-delay: -0.1s; --cg-meteor-dur: 1.5s; --cg-meteor-len: 32px; --cg-meteor-thick: 4px; }

.cg-model-meteor:nth-child(2) { --cg-meteor-y: 8px; --cg-meteor-delay: -0.5s; --cg-meteor-dur: 1.9s; --cg-meteor-len: 26px; --cg-meteor-thick: 4px; }

.cg-model-meteor:nth-child(3) { --cg-meteor-y: 13px; --cg-meteor-delay: -0.88s; --cg-meteor-dur: 2.1s; --cg-meteor-len: 20px; --cg-meteor-thick: 3px; }

.cg-model-meteor:nth-child(4) { --cg-meteor-y: 18px; --cg-meteor-delay: -1.26s; --cg-meteor-dur: 1.7s; --cg-meteor-len: 34px; --cg-meteor-thick: 4px; }

.cg-model-meteor:nth-child(5) { --cg-meteor-y: 17px; --cg-meteor-delay: -1.62s; --cg-meteor-dur: 2.3s; --cg-meteor-len: 23px; --cg-meteor-thick: 4px; }

.cg-model-meteor:nth-child(6) { --cg-meteor-y: 6px; --cg-meteor-delay: -1.98s; --cg-meteor-dur: 2.6s; --cg-meteor-len: 16px; --cg-meteor-thick: 3px; }

.cg-model-meteor:nth-child(7) { --cg-meteor-y: 11px; --cg-meteor-delay: -0.3s; --cg-meteor-dur: 1.6s; --cg-meteor-len: 28px; --cg-meteor-thick: 4px; }

.cg-model-meteor:nth-child(8) { --cg-meteor-y: 15px; --cg-meteor-delay: -1.14s; --cg-meteor-dur: 2s; --cg-meteor-len: 24px; --cg-meteor-thick: 4px; }

.cg-model-meteor:nth-child(9) { --cg-meteor-y: 15px; --cg-meteor-delay: -0.68s; --cg-meteor-dur: 1.8s; --cg-meteor-len: 18px; --cg-meteor-thick: 3px; }

.cg-model-meteor:nth-child(10) { --cg-meteor-y: 5px; --cg-meteor-delay: -1.44s; --cg-meteor-dur: 2.2s; --cg-meteor-len: 30px; --cg-meteor-thick: 4px; }

